  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 401 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe dry and cool post-frontal conditions seen this weekend will \ngradually warm and moisten toward the start of next week. A broad \nsurface high over the eastern United States will veers winds to a \nmore northeasterly flow, advecting warm moist air from the Atlantic \nwaters. Thanks to this influx of moisture, temperatures and \ndewpoints will begin to climb going into early next week. High temps \nin the upper 70s to low 80s will return along with dewpoints in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. \n\nHeading into the middle of next week, a northern jet appears to set \nup aloft across North Florida. This jet could pull along with it a \nshortwave trough that is forecast to develop over the southern \nPlains, resulting in an increase in PoPs and surface winds for parts \nof SoFlo. Guidance agrees on the eastward propagation of this \nmid- level shortwave feature approaching the Florida peninsula \naround midweek. An associated surface low will develop and move \neast along with the parent mid-level shortwave. As this low nears \nFlorida and interacts with the surface high settled over the \neastern CONUS, a gradual tightening the pressure gradient over the\nSoFlo region will take place. Placement and intensity of the \nsurface low will depend on the strength and positioning of the \nshortwave trough. Currently, GEFS, GEPS and EPS guidance suggest a\nweaker and more southerly track for the shortwave, which would \npush the associated surface low further south of the SoFlo region.\nFurther analysis of these ensemble systems show some split within\neach model's member envelope. The GEFS members appear split on \nwhether the surface low will develop over the Gulf or over the \ncoastal Carolina waters, which is also supported by the EPS \nensemble membership. \n\nWith all of these potential solutions to consider, uncertainty \nregarding the resulting sensible weather across South Florida mid-to-\nlate next week remains high. What is more certain is that a tighter \npressure gradient will increase surface winds early next week, \nparticularly over coastal waters, calling for a gusty week ahead \nwith the potential for hazardous marine conditions. Higher rain \nchances will be likely throughout the later half next week \nregardless of the positioning of the surface low. Activity should \nmostly be confined to the eastern metros as typical coastal \nconvergence sets up around mid-week. However, given the scenario \nwhere the surface low sets up near the Carolinas rather than over \nthe eastern Gulf, increased coastal convergence and moisture \ntransport could increase rain chances.",
